import{_ as a,d as t,e as n,x as i}from"./chunks/framework.BRr8VP3a.js";const h=JSON.parse('{"title":"D.26 Networks, Media, Language, Narrative, and Identification","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"D.26 Networks, Media, Language, Narrative, and Identification","xkChars":0,"xkWords":508},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-26.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-26.md"}'),o={name:"en/theory/appendix-d/d-26.md"};function s(r,e,c,d,l,p){return t(),n("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<div class="theory-source"><h1 id="section-d-26" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">D.26</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Networks, Media, Language, Narrative, and Identification <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-d-26" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;D.26 Networks, Media, Language, Narrative, and Identification {#section-d-26}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>In the main text, see §5.5.3, §5.10, §9.7, §10–11, §15.3.4, and §15.3.6. Research entries cover 26.01–26.12.</p><p>Networks can create opportunities. Language can make experience intelligible, narratives can help people mourn and discuss responsibility, and identity can support cooperation. Analysis should retain these benefits and distinguish ties, actual help, exposure, reading, beliefs, public expression, and action. Weak ties, bridging positions, structural holes, centrality, and resources that can actually be mobilized are distinct concepts.</p><p>Teaching example. Each of two groups consists of three people who are all connected within the group. A single edge connects the groups. If people act after receiving a prompt from one neighbor who has already acted, the behavior may spread across the edge. If support from two distinct neighbors is necessary, receiving the message may be insufficient. The cumulative success probability for repeated independent attempts, 1−(1−p)^n, is distinct from social reinforcement. Several social nodes can offer support while sharing the same factual source.</p><p>Silence can stem from risk, privacy, lack of exposure, or lack of interest. It cannot automatically be taken as approval or opposition. Being unverified, being demonstrably false, and being deliberately deceptive are distinct states. Strong collective emotion need not be irrational, and actual harm does not make an identity group responsible for all of it. Clear boundaries do not imply that a group is closed, and identity fusion or costly behavior does not automatically establish free consent.</p><p>In translation, distinguish words and sentences, the message as a whole, the audience’s task, and the authority to represent others. Language proficiency, listeners’ understanding, institutional access, and the quality of the content each have their own channels. Close reading of narratives can explain experience and meaning without substituting for technical causal analysis. Conversely, identifying a technical cause does not make the work of memory and literature redundant. Facts learned later cannot be treated as facts the narrator already knew on the day of the account.</p><p>Keep track of the actual treatment version when studying a platform. Changes to the feed, a ranking weight, content matching, and author feedback cannot share a single treatment label. A fixed recommendation rule can still generate feedback through human responses. Retraining the model is unnecessary for some kinds of feedback. Evaluate changes to system-maintained profiles on their own, and assess private preferences, actual opportunities, and long-term harm separately. Repetition of the same original factual source does not negate the social coordination that multiple supporters may produce.</p><p>Research topics. 26.01 Weak ties, structural holes, and network resources (Granovetter and Burt). 26.02 Diffusion, thresholds, and complex contagion, with Granovetter, Watts, and Centola. 26.03 Social identity, self-categorization, and group boundaries (Tajfel and John Turner). 26.04 Agenda-setting, framing, and media selection. 26.05 Silence, spirals, rumors, and misinformation. 26.06 Narrative, memory, metaphor, and public explanation. 26.07 Attention on platforms, recommendation, and public opinion. 26.08 Translation across languages and the transfer of concepts. 26.09 Language learning, language institutions, and inequality in expression. 26.10 Uncertainty–identity and optimal distinctiveness. 26.11 Collective emotion, terror management, and moral panic. 26.12 Identity fusion and costly shared experiences.</p></div>',1)])])}const m=a(o,[["render",s]]);export{h as __pageData,m as default};
